  "query_preview": "..A closed low is located across northern Arizona\nwhich is keeping showers with some embedded thunderstorms ongoing\nacross southern and eastern Utah. This low will slowly meander to\nthe northeast today and tomorrow with a similar threat for\nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms, but rainfall rates\nwill generally remain under 0.4\"/hour limiting the flash flood\nthreat to the heaviest cores in the most sensitive locations.\nTemperatures will remain below normal over the next couple of days\nwith highs in the 50s across much of the area with 60s in the\nlower terrain of southern Utah. Overnight lows will also be cold\nwith 30s and 40s areawide and patchy frost developing across many\nvalley locations tonight and tomorrow. \n\nShortwave ridging on Monday will bring a brief pause to the\nunsettled weather before a trough rotating across the PNW grazes\nnorthern Utah with a cold front Monday night. This will bring a\nquick round of precipitation with valley rain and snow levels\ngenerally above 7000 feet. The precipitation will be short-lived\nso amounts will generally between <0.25\" and confined to areas\nalong the Wasatch front and points to the east. This will also\nbring temperatures back down a few degrees below normal before\ngradually warming throughout the week.\n\nBehind the front on Monday flow will stay zonal with a lull in\nactivity before another dynamic moisture rich system approaches\nthe west coast towards the end of the week. Guidance has pretty\ngood consensus on a trough moving into the PNW with an active\nsouthern jet stream pulling in moisture into the southwest. This\nwill bring a return to widespread precipitation by the end of the\nweek and into the weekend, but the heaviest precipitation looks to\nremain across Arizona.",
